  "readme": [
    {
      "section": "dsh-ui-notify",
      "text": "Completion sound notifications for the [DeepSeek Harness](https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness) web GUI. When a session's output finishes, the plugin plays an embedded chime; the **Notifications** row in **Settings → General** turns it on or off, picks a sound (with preview), sets the volume, and chooses whether to ring for background sessions only or every session."
    },
    {
      "section": "dsh-ui-notify",
      "text": "Settings persist in browser `localStorage`. The plugin is a pure web plugin — it registers no host service, no tool, and no session-log entry."
    },
    {
      "section": "Install",
      "text": "The package is published to the npm registry. Install it into the `web` profile:\n\n```sh\ndsh plugin --profile web add dsh-ui-notify\n```\n\nThen restart the profile (`dsh --profile web`) so the new package metadata is picked up. No build step is needed — the published tarball ships the built `lib/` (node half + browser client bundle + type declarations).\n\nTo update to a newer release:\n\n```sh\ndsh plugin --profile web up dsh-ui-notify\n```"
    },
    {
      "section": "Configuration",
      "text": "All settings live under the **Notifications** row (General settings), stored in `localStorage` under `dsh-ui-notify.settings`:\n\n| Field | Values | Default |\n|---|---|---|\n| Enable reminders | on / off | on |\n| Sound | None, Chime 1–3 | Chime 1 |\n| Volume | 0–100% | 50% |\n| Remind when | Background session finishes / Any session finishes | Background |"
    },
    {
      "section": "Configuration",
      "text": "The first user gesture unlocks the audio (browser autoplay policy); a completion that rang before unlock is queued and played on the next gesture."
    },
    {
      "section": "Development",
      "text": "```sh\npnpm install\npnpm run typecheck\npnpm run test\npnpm run build\n\n`pnpm run build` runs the two-project TypeScript build (node half + browser half) then bundles `lib/client.js` with tsdown. `pnpm run prepare` is the self-contained consumer build: it transpiles both halves from source without the type-checked peer graph, for installs that build from source rather than the npm tarball."
    },
    {
      "section": "License",
      "text": "MIT"
    }
  ],
